             optional ndigits and read a NEGATIVE one as tens and
             hundreds (round(1234, -2) = 1200), which is exactly what
             83.9(a) shipped -- including round(1.005, 2) = 1.00, for
             the same binary reason.

             THE ONE SYSTEMATIC DIVERGENCE, which is about SEMANTICS
             and not names, and is deliberate on both sides: abacus
             RAISES where this language returns a value. abacus
             refuses sqrt(-1) ("square root of a negative value") and
             1/0 ("float division by zero"); here both are values that
             travel on (83.18), because a gap in one sample of a curve
             must not take the whole plate down with it, and abacus is
             a calculator answering one question at a time where this
             is a language evaluated per message and per sample. The
             same split covers log(0), the reciprocal-trig poles and
             asin(2). Where we DO refuse -- factorial, gcd, lcm
             (83.14) -- we refuse the same arguments abacus does.

             NAMES ABACUS HAS AND WE DO NOT, each with its entry:
             avg/median/stddev/variance and the variadic min/max/gcd/
             lcm (83.21a and 83.21b), sum/product/diff/integral
             (83.21c), time (83.17). One shape difference beside
             those: abacus spells its constants `pi()` and `e()` as
             zero-argument calls as well as bare names, and this
             language has NO zero-argument call at all (81.3c), so
             `pi` and `e` are bare only.

